3. Is the pin load-bearing? (it is)

  $ cat rust-toolchain.toml        -> channel = "1.98.1", components clippy+rustfmt, target x86_64-unknown-none
  $ cat kernel/rust-toolchain.toml -> same
  $ cargo -V                       -> cargo 1.98.1 (797e8a9bc 2026-08-05)

CI proves the pin actually overrides the runner's default. The workflow installs
`stable` (rustc 1.99.1), and rustup then switches to the pinned channel:

  docs/logs/c5-ci-41441331339.log:679  ... stable-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu updated - rustc 1.99.1
  docs/logs/c5-ci-41441331339.log:718  ... info: syncing channel updates for 1.98.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
  docs/logs/c5-ci-41441331339.log:719  ... info: latest update on 2026-09-03 for version 1.98.1 (48a229cea 2026-09-01)
  docs/logs/c5-ci-41441331339.log:721  ... warn: the missing active toolchain `1.98.1-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` has been auto-installed

4. PLANT 1 (mine, not the author's): a non-deterministic build input.
   Committed in the scratch clone only, as 2209cfd "REVIEW PLANT: build-time
   timestamp input". scripts/build-disk.sh gains, before the kernel build:

     if [ "${REVIEW_PLANT:-0}" = "1" ]; then
       printf '\nconst _RV: u64 = %s;\n' "$(date +%s%N)" >> "$KERNEL_DIR/src/main.rs"
       sed -i 's/sprintln!("NOVA_BOOT_OK");/sprintln!("NOVA_BOOT_OK {}", _RV);/' "$KERNEL_DIR/src/main.rs"
     fi

   Each extraction then embeds its own build time. This is true non-determinism:
   the tree is the same, the host is the same, only the clock differs.

  $ REVIEW_PLANT=1 bash scripts/repro-check.sh ; echo $?
  kernel ELF 1 fbcd7da33aa7c451cf73c6eabf47f5d9b1d1847e0789e5d74d8ae8186cd2448f  222504 bytes
  kernel ELF 2 6e803c094992e3eaf636722d7fd651d300c375ed46a6ca2183573db373169425  222504 bytes
  nova.hdd   1 00a5c66eb75d6741c401ed5763d257287418a65cfcb98d840cefe93220202aec  41943040 bytes
  nova.hdd   2 80d60275b1998c4ab7a80b7370aedf7659210fe98c8514aac216759da069778f  41943040 bytes

  DIFFER: kernel ELF
  $ cmp -l <elf1> <elf2> | head
  149297   0 100
  149298 273  64
  149299   4 167
  149300 324 330
  149301 130 132
  DIFFER: nova.hdd
  ...
  RESULT: builds differ; non-determinism is a finding, not a pass
  exit status 1

   The check has teeth on same-host non-determinism. This plant goes red.

5. PLANT 2 (mine): remove `--remap-path-prefix=src\=src/` from
   kernel/.cargo/config.toml, committed in the scratch clone.
   This is exactly the regression C5 exists to prevent: without the remap, a
   Windows build embeds backslash paths and a Linux build embeds slash paths.

   (a) the remap is load-bearing. Removing it changes the Windows hash:

  $ rm -rf kernel/target build/nova.hdd && bash scripts/build-disk.sh release
  build rc=0
  $ sha256sum kernel/target/x86_64-unknown-none/release/nucleus build/nova.hdd
  44429f0109727a7590304964a4d173506897f8862003327783eb09ceea1a2a1e *kernel/target/.../nucleus
  33e73a23a707aeffa1178521d7b4652fc14e5b60a74364d1d685258d961aa725 *build/nova.hdd
  (with the remap: 83720965... / a7d41aa4...)

   (b) the mechanism is file paths, as the author said:

  $ grep -aoE "src[\\/][a-z0-9_]+\.rs" .../nucleus | sort | uniq -c | head
        1 src\capselftest.rs
        1 src\console.rs
        1 src\draw.rs
        1 src\elf.rs
        1 src\elfselftest.rs
        1 src\excselftest.rs
        1 src\fat.rs
        1 src\fatselftest.rs
        1 src\font.rs
        1 src\framebuffer.rs

   (c) THE FINDING. With the remap removed, the CI gate still passes:

  $ bash scripts/repro-check.sh ; echo $?
  kernel ELF 1 44429f0109727a7590304964a4d173506897f8862003327783eb09ceea1a2a1e  222504 bytes
  kernel ELF 2 44429f0109727a7590304964a4d173506897f8862003327783eb09ceea1a2a1e  222504 bytes
  IDENTICAL: kernel ELF
  IDENTICAL: nova.hdd
  RESULT: reproducible (both kernel ELF and nova.hdd match)
  exit status 0

   scripts/repro-check.sh compares two builds on ONE host, from the same
   `git archive HEAD`. It cannot see a host-dependent difference, so deleting
   the remap would make the Windows and Linux artefacts diverge again while
   every CI run stays green. Nothing in the repo compares a Windows hash with a
   Linux hash automatically, and the kernel hash is not pinned anywhere a
   machine can check: tools/manifest/bootchain.sha256 pins the vendored Limine
   files and the font, not the kernel ELF. (Linux behaviour without the remap is
   inferred, not run: CI is the only Linux host and I did not push the plant
   there. On Linux `src\` never occurs in a path, so the remap is a no-op there
   and the Linux hash is unchanged; the divergence would therefore reopen.)

6. Close read of the one check under review: what is on each side, and where
   did each come from?
   Left:  $DIR1/kernel/target/x86_64-unknown-none/release/nucleus and
          $DIR1/build/nova.hdd, built by scripts/build-disk.sh inside DIR1.
   Right: the same two paths inside $DIR2, built by the same script.
   Both sides trace to `git -C "$ROOT" archive HEAD | tar -x -C <dir>` -- the
   same commit, the same toolchain, the same host, the same OS, the same clock
   zone, the same Limine binaries (vendored, committed, so no network: I checked
   with a clean `git archive` extraction, fetch-limine.sh takes the vendored
   branch -- "fetch-limine: using vendored Limine (.../.freebuff/ref/limine/
   limine-binary)"). The only independent variable on each side is the
   extraction path, which is what a per-host reproducibility check is for.
   So this is not a self-comparison of a value against its own source: the two
   artefacts are produced by two separate compilations. It IS a same-host-only
   check, which is finding F1 below.

