FINDINGS

F1 (high, not a false claim). scripts/repro-check.sh cannot detect cross-host
   non-determinism. With --remap-path-prefix removed, the build becomes
   host-dependent (Windows 44429f01... vs 83720965...), yet the check reports
   "IDENTICAL" and exits 0 (section 5c). The gate therefore guards the symptom
   that was already fixed, not the class of bug C5 was about. A regression that
   deletes the remap would be caught by nobody. Suggested fix, not applied:
   commit the expected kernel ELF and nova.hdd digests (tools/manifest/
   bootchain.sha256 already exists for exactly this) and have repro-check
   compare both builds against those, so any host's build can be graded; or add
   a windows-latest job. The cross-host equality itself is real (section 2) --
   it is enforced once by hand, not by CI.

F2 (low, a false statement in a comment). scripts/repro-check.sh claims
   "SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH must be an integer ... an explicit value is honoured" and
   exports it, but nothing in the build reads it:
     $ grep -rn "SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" scripts tools kernel mathd .github
   returns only scripts/repro-check.sh itself (6 lines). The build contains no
   date at all, which is why it is deterministic; the variable is decorative.
   The comment should say so, or the build should consume it.

F3 (informational). `repro-check.sh --plant` plants an *input change* (a fixed
   timestamp written into one copy), not a non-deterministic input. It proves
   only that the script notices different sources. It cannot distinguish that
   from real non-determinism; plant 1 above is the real test.

F4 (process). The pin resolves through rustup's auto-install of channel 1.98.1
   (CI log line 721). That is reproducible only while 1.98.1 stays published --
   which Rust guarantees for released versions -- but the CI workflow still
   installs `stable` first (line 679 shows 1.99.1), i.e. it downloads a
   toolchain it does not use. Harmless, but confusing; the "Install toolchains"
   step should install the pinned channel.

VERDICT: PASS
  The toolchain pin is real and enforced by rustup (CI log proves 1.99.1 is
  replaced by 1.98.1). Windows and Linux really do produce the same kernel ELF
  (83720965c9becd8efaefee6bae2482cb9b663bf3881cb164c4e206faf3ff7204, 222,504
  bytes) and the same nova.hdd
  (a7d41aa459525bf34180a4e2edbebfda4c984b7ba6522b9df7cf3f0e37440dee, 41,943,040
  bytes): I reproduced the Windows side myself and compared against the live CI
  log from GitHub. The remap is load-bearing and its mechanism is the backslash
  paths the author described. Findings F1-F4 stand, and F1 should be fixed
  before anyone relies on the gate to protect cross-host reproducibility.
